## 3. Core Module A: Advanced Band Structure Analysis

Develop an interactive band-structure explorer for conventional, spin-polarized, magnetic, and spin-orbit-coupled systems.

### Features
- Plot eigenvalues along arbitrary reciprocal-space paths.
- Display high-symmetry-point labels and path segments.
- Support fractional and Cartesian reciprocal coordinates.
- Set and compare Fermi-energy references.
- Select individual bands and energy windows.
- Highlight selected bands and candidate crossings.
- Compare multiple calculations on the same axes.
- Display spin-up and spin-down channels where physically appropriate.
- Display spin expectation values using colors, symbols, or vector representations when available.
- Display orbital, atomic-site, and element projections when supported.
- Export plots and underlying numerical data.

### Physics-aware analysis
Identify and characterize candidate:
- Dirac points.
- Weyl points when sufficient three-dimensional information is available.
- Nodal-line or nodal-loop features.
- Band crossings and avoided crossings.
- Flat and weakly dispersive bands.
- Band inversions and changes in band ordering.
- Symmetry-related band degeneracies.
- Spin splitting and SOC-induced band gaps.

Every automatically identified feature should include its estimated location, energy, numerical evidence, detection method, and confidence or verification status.

Do not infer topological protection solely from an apparent crossing in a plotted band structure.

## 4. Core Module B: Kagome Materials Analyzer

Create a specialized analysis workspace for kagome-lattice materials.

The module should help researchers investigate the characteristic electronic structures associated with kagome geometry and its interactions with magnetism, spin-orbit coupling, and electronic correlations.

### B1. Flat-band detection

Identify candidate flat or weakly dispersive bands.

Calculate and report:
- Bandwidth over a specified k-space region.
- Energy range of the band.
- Dispersion along selected reciprocal-space directions.
- Band index and energy relative to the Fermi level.
- Sensitivity to the chosen energy reference and k-point path.
- Available orbital or atomic projections.

Allow users to select a bandwidth threshold, while clearly labeling the threshold as a user-defined heuristic rather than a universal physical criterion.

Distinguish a genuinely weakly dispersive band over a well-sampled region from a band that merely appears flat along a short high-symmetry path.

### B2. Dirac-point detection

Search for candidate Dirac crossings in the supplied band data.

Provide:
- Estimated k-point coordinates.
- Crossing energy.
- Candidate band indices.
- Approximate energy separation between bands.
- Local dispersion in available directions.
- Evidence of symmetry-related crossings.
- Comparison between calculations with and without SOC.

Where sufficiently dense local data are available, estimate linear dispersion and anisotropy around the crossing.

Distinguish a candidate Dirac point from an avoided crossing or an accidental degeneracy. Do not claim symmetry protection without the relevant symmetry analysis.

### B3. Van Hove singularities

Analyze total and projected density of states for peaks that may be associated with van Hove singularities.

Provide:
- Peak energy.
- Peak height and width under the stated numerical broadening.
- Orbital and atomic projections where available.
- Relation to nearby bands and candidate saddle points when sufficient k-space information is supplied.

Treat a DOS peak as a candidate feature, not definitive proof of a van Hove singularity.

### B4. Kagome-specific visualization

Provide a combined view of:
- Band structures.
- Total and projected DOS.
- Candidate flat bands.
- Candidate Dirac points.
- Fermi-surface features.
- Spin splitting.
- SOC-induced changes.

Support comparisons between different magnetic configurations, structures, or computational settings.

## 5. Core Module C: Altermagnet Analysis

Develop a dedicated module for investigating altermagnetic electronic structures and related magnetic-symmetry phenomena.

The objective is to help researchers characterize momentum-dependent spin splitting and evaluate whether their calculations are consistent with an altermagnetic interpretation.

### C1. Spin-resolved band structures

Support:
- Collinear spin-polarized calculations without SOC.
- Spin-polarized calculations with SOC.
- Noncollinear magnetic calculations.
- Spin expectation values resolved by band and k-point.
- Comparison of magnetic configurations.
- Comparison of calculations with and without Hubbard U, where supplied.

Display spin information using:
- Spin-channel colors for suitable collinear calculations.
- Color maps for the expectation value of a selected spin component.
- Vector or component-resolved representations for noncollinear calculations.
- Selectable energy and reciprocal-space windows.

Do not equate spin-up/down channel labels with a universal physical spin direction when the calculation uses noncollinear magnetism or SOC.

### C2. Momentum-dependent spin splitting

Calculate the energy separation between relevant bands at the same k-point.

Provide:
- Spin-resolved energy differences.
- Maximum and representative splitting values within a defined k-space region.
- Momentum-dependent splitting maps.
- Splitting along user-selected high-symmetry paths.
- Comparisons between magnetic configurations.
- Optional symmetry-related k-point comparisons.

Explain the convention used to match bands. Band indices alone may not provide reliable correspondence near degeneracies or band crossings.

### C3. Spin texture

When spin expectation values are available, visualize:

- Sx(k), Sy(k), and Sz(k).
- In-plane spin textures.
- Out-of-plane spin components.
- Spin textures on selected bands.
- Spin textures on constant-energy surfaces when the required data is available.

Support spin expectation values obtained from appropriate calculations or post-processing tools.

Do not reconstruct a physical spin texture from ordinary eigenvalues alone.

### C4. Magnetic symmetry and classification

Allow users to supply or identify:
- Crystal structure.
- Magnetic space group when known.
- Magnetic ordering.
- Moment directions.
- Magnetic symmetry operations.
- Relevant computational settings.

Provide symmetry-aware diagnostics for expected band degeneracies, allowed spin splitting, and symmetry-related momentum points.

Where feasible, integrate magnetic symmetry analysis and magnetic space-group identification.

Distinguish a candidate altermagnetic electronic structure from a confirmed altermagnetic classification. Spin splitting alone is not sufficient to establish altermagnetism.

Clearly state when additional information about magnetic order, symmetry, or the calculation setup is required.

## 6. Core Module D: Spin-Orbit Coupling Analysis

Create a workflow for comparing calculations performed with and without SOC.

### Features
- Align band structures using a documented energy-reference convention.
- Compare eigenvalues at equivalent k-points.
- Detect candidate SOC-induced gaps.
- Identify changes in band ordering.
- Highlight changes in degeneracies and avoided crossings.
- Compare band dispersions near the Fermi level.
- Compare spin expectation values where meaningful.
- Compare DOS before and after SOC.
- Identify changes in candidate Dirac, Weyl, or nodal features.

### Quantitative analysis

For selected bands and k-space regions, calculate:
- Energy shifts.
- Candidate gap openings.
- Changes in local dispersion.
- Changes in band ordering.
- Changes in Fermi-level crossings.
- Changes in spin expectation values.

Require compatible structures, reciprocal-space coordinates, computational settings, and band correspondence for meaningful comparisons.

Flag comparisons where different structures, k-point paths, energy references, or calculation settings make direct interpretation unreliable.

Do not infer a topological phase transition merely from an SOC-induced gap. Topological invariants or other suitable evidence may be required.

## 7. Core Module E: Fermi Surface Analysis

Develop an interactive Fermi-surface explorer for three-dimensional and two-dimensional electronic structures.

### Features
- Visualize constant-energy surfaces.
- Select the Fermi energy or another energy of interest.
- Display individual bands or selected band groups.
- Color surfaces by band index, velocity, spin expectation value, or other available quantities.
- Display spin-resolved surfaces when appropriate.
- Inspect high-symmetry points and reciprocal-space boundaries.
- Generate two-dimensional k-space slices.
- Export images, mesh data, and numerical data where supported.

### Scientific requirements

Distinguish between:
- Fermi surfaces reconstructed from a sampled three-dimensional eigenvalue grid.
- Approximate surfaces generated from interpolated band energies.
- Apparent crossings seen only along a high-symmetry path.

A high-symmetry band path alone is insufficient to reconstruct a complete Fermi surface.

Support dense k-space grids or reliable interpolation methods when available. Warn about insufficient k-point density, incomplete Brillouin-zone coverage, and interpolation uncertainty.

For spin-split systems, support separate sheets and band-resolved visualization without assuming that spin channels remain independent when SOC is present.

## 8. Core Module F: Berry Curvature and Anomalous Hall Conductivity

Build an advanced module for investigating Berry curvature and anomalous Hall conductivity (AHC).

This module is a high-priority research feature, but it requires more specialized input data and validation than ordinary band-structure plotting.

### F1. Berry curvature visualization

Support compatible Berry-curvature data from suitable electronic-structure and Wannier-based workflows.

Provide:
- Berry-curvature maps in selected k-space planes.
- Three-dimensional k-space slices when available.
- Band-resolved Berry curvature.
- Occupied-state or summed contributions where supported.
- Energy-resolved analysis where supplied data permits it.
- Identification of regions with large positive or negative contributions.
- Comparison between calculations with and without SOC.
- Export of numerical grids and publication-quality plots.

Display the component being plotted, its units or normalization convention, the k-space coordinates, and the integration settings.

Do not infer Berry curvature from ordinary band eigenvalues alone.

### F2. Anomalous Hall conductivity

Support AHC calculations from compatible data produced by validated Kubo-formula, Berry-curvature, Wannier interpolation, or equivalent implementations.

Provide:
- Conductivity tensor components.
- AHC versus Fermi energy.
- AHC versus k-space sampling density where data is available.
- Comparisons between magnetic configurations.
- Comparisons between calculations with and without SOC.
- Band-resolved contributions where supported.
- Convergence diagnostics.
- Exportable tables and figures.

Display tensor components with explicit indices and units. For three-dimensional bulk conductivity, commonly used units include S/cm; for two-dimensional systems, document the normalization convention and units used by the source.

### F3. AHC validation

Check, where the necessary information is supplied:
- k-point grid density.
- Brillouin-zone integration coverage.
- Fermi-energy reference.
- Energy and broadening parameters.
- Convergence with sampling density.
- Treatment of occupied states.
- Consistency of units and tensor conventions.
- Agreement between related calculations or post-processing methods.

Clearly distinguish a value read from an existing output file from a value independently recalculated by QuantumLens.

Do not silently convert units or change conductivity sign conventions.

A large Berry-curvature hotspot does not necessarily imply a large net AHC because contributions from different regions and bands can cancel.

Do not report AHC as a fully validated result if the available inputs are insufficient to verify the integration and convergence.

